  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 247 AM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nAs mentioned above, a digging trough across the Great Lakes \nwill send a weak, dry cold front into the area late Sunday, which \nwill only act to slow our weekend warmup briefly Monday. Behind this \nfront, another expansive area of surface high pressure will shove \nany appreciable moisture even further toward the Gulf, and reinforce \na dry, continental airmass across the area. This should drop \ntemperatures by roughly 5 degrees or so Monday and switch winds to \nthe north briefly, but otherwise, its effect on sensible weather \nwill be hardly noticeable. \n\nFrom Tuesday onward, though, ensemble guidance continues to suggest \nthat a broad upper ridge across the western 2/3rds of the CONUS \nwill slowly expand eastward, resulting in gradually building \ngeopotential heights aloft. Meanwhile, weak south to southwest low\nlevel flow will resume as the second iteration of high pressure \nmoves eastward, and continues sunny skies will allow for several \ndays of nearly full insolation. As such, a steady warming trend is\nexpected through the rest of the work week, and this is supported\nby ensemble temperature forecasts. Confidence is high that we \nwill once again reach well above average (50-80% above 80 degrees \nWednesday onward), but we do note that the NBM ensemble suite is \nonce again a bit on the warm side of available guidance, and it \nmay be the result of a slightly overaggressive bias correction. \nStill, we're talking about a difference of 2-4 degrees, and at \nthese temperature ranges this isn't a particularly impactful \ndifference at these temperature ranges.\n\nFinally, due to the continued influence of a very dry airmass and \nhigh pressure, precipitation chances are almost nonexistent \nthroughout the work week.",
